```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
class OrderController {
    private final OrderService service;

    OrderController(OrderService service) {
        this.service = service;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    OrderView find(@PathVariable long id) {
        return service.find(id);
    }

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<OrderView> create(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request) {
        OrderView created = service.create(request);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/orders/" + created.id()))
                             .body(created);
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    OrderView replace(@PathVariable long id, @RequestBody ReplaceOrderRequest request) {
        return service.replace(id, request);
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    void delete(@PathVariable long id) {
        service.delete(id);
    }
}
```

The annotations expose the intended method. The service must still make PUT and DELETE idempotent and keep GET free of requested state changes.

